<%- include('partials/header', { title: 'Studio Admin FAQ' }) %>

  <main class="container">
    <h1>Studio Admin FAQ & Instructions</h1>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>1. What is AwardHome, and why does it matter to my studio?</h2>
      <p>AwardHome hosts curated digital trophy cases for competitive-dance families: results from competitions
      across the country live on as the permanent portfolios of thousands of dancers and studios — including
      yours. Every award card carries the studio the routine competed with — on the front face above the event
      line and on the certificate back — and cards get shared and screenshotted by dancers and parents, so your
      studio's name goes along with every win. See for yourself:</p>
      <%- include('partials/demo_card', { demo: {
            studioName: 'Your Studio Here',
            ackMessage: "Thank you Coach Marina and my studio family — this one's for all of us!" } }) %>
      <p>Once you <strong>claim your studio</strong>, your studio page becomes the doorway those shared wins lead
      to: a screenshot carries your studio's name, and anyone who looks that name up on AwardHome lands on a page
      you've made your own — your logo, your bio, your links. Most importantly, claiming lets
      you complete your studio's record: you can <strong>add awards that are missing</strong> from your page and
      <strong>add your dancers to the group routines they danced in</strong> (most competitions never publish
      those names, so those wins sit uncredited until you connect them). Every dancer you connect gets their own
      award cards to share — and every one of those shared cards carries your studio's achievements further.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>2. How do I claim my studio?</h2>
      <p>Search for your studio name on the homepage. Click on
        your studio, and then click the "Claim Studio" button next to the title. Once approved by our team, you will
        receive full access to the Management Dashboard.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>3. What is the AwardHome &ldquo;Studio Secret Code&rdquo; my dancers are asking about?</h2>
      <p>That's your studio's private claim code — it appears on your dashboard as the
      <strong>Award Claim Code</strong> card (Manage Studio page). When a dancer family claims an award or links
      their dancer to your studio, entering the code proves they really belong to your studio — it's what keeps
      your roster in your control.</p>
      <p>Why does the code exist at all? Many competitions don't publish dancer names for group routines — and some
      no longer have the data — so we often can't add those &ldquo;missing&rdquo; award-winning dancers to your
      studio ourselves. But the dancers usually remember exactly which routines they danced in: with your code,
      they add themselves to those routines, and each self-addition or correction lands in your admin dashboard
      for you to confirm — so you can make sure the kids didn't make any mistakes.</p>
      <p>Share it privately with your families — the class app, a newsletter, a note home — rather than anywhere
      public. If it ever leaks, hit <strong>Reset Code</strong> next to it: a new code takes effect immediately
      and the old one stops working.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>4. How do I customize my public profile?</h2>
      <p>From the Management Dashboard, you can update your public profile. This includes:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Uploading a Studio Logo</li>
        <li>Writing a Bio</li>
        <li>Linking your Instagram and TikTok handles</li>
      </ul>
      <p>These details will immediately reflect on your public studio page, helping dancers and parents find your
        official links.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>5. How do I add dancers to my roster?</h2>
      <p>Besides your dancers adding or confirming themselves to your studio (with your Award Claim Code — see
        question 3), you can also add them yourself using their <strong>Unique ID</strong>.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Ask the dancer (or their parent) for their Unique ID, which they can find on their public profile URL or
          inside their Dancer Dashboard.</li>
        <li>Go to the <strong>Dancer Roster</strong> tab in your Management Dashboard.</li>
        <li>Click <strong>+ Add/Claim Dancer</strong> and enter the Unique ID. This instantly links their existing
          awards to your studio.</li>
        <li>If the dancer does not exist in the system yet, you can use the same popup to instantly create a new profile
          for them!</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>6. My group awards show no dancers — how do I add the cast?</h2>
      <p>Most competitions never publish who danced in group routines, so imported group awards
        arrive without dancer names. Open <strong>Check Routine Dancers</strong> in your management sidebar (the amber count shows how many are waiting): every
        routine with uncredited awards is listed by year, and you can paste the cast for each one — one name per line or
        comma-separated. Click <strong>Preview</strong> to see exactly what will happen before anything saves:
        names already on your roster get linked (with a checkbox to untick if the routine's dancer is
        actually a different dancer who shares the name), new names create dancer profiles on your
        roster, and if two of your dancers share a name we'll ask which one danced it — each
        candidate shows their recent routines, and you can give each dancer a <strong>private
        tag</strong> (like "Senior Mia") right there or on your Roster page to tell them apart
        in your own words. Tags are only visible in your management tools, never publicly. Confirming applies the cast to
        <strong>every award that routine won that year</strong>, and each dancer's own trophy case updates
        instantly.</p>
      <p><strong>Did the lineup change between events?</strong> When a routine won awards at more than one event,
        the card lists each event with a checkbox (all ticked by default — one paste covers everything). If a
        dancer missed an event or a sub stepped in, untick the events where the cast differed, save this round,
        and the card stays flagged with "missing dancers" on the remaining events so you can enter their lineup
        separately. And when some events already have the cast and others don't, <strong>Sync dancers across
        ticked events</strong> copies everyone over in one click — additions only, nobody is ever removed, and
        dancers you removed from an award stay removed. And for a browsable index of everything —
        every routine, year, and the dancers on file, sortable and searchable — open
        <strong>All Routines</strong> in the sidebar.</p>
      <p><strong>Same routine spelled differently?</strong> We automatically fold capitalization and
        punctuation differences ("Tides Of/of Reunion"), and each event row shows the title exactly as that
        competition published it, so you can see the grouping is right. For true misspellings ("Kongfu" vs
        "Kungfu Kiddies"), tick the rows on <strong>All Routines</strong> and use <strong>Merge / fix
        spelling</strong> — you choose the correct spelling, everything merges under it, and it's undoable
        anytime from the same page. The imported names are never altered.</p>
      <p><strong>Nothing actually missing?</strong> Routines leave the check list automatically once every event
        has dancers, and routines with dancers can be ticked off with <strong>✓ Mark complete</strong> (undoable
        from "Show all routines" or the All Routines page). Routines where no name was ever recorded stay on the
        list — those dancers deserve their credit! — but after a few seasons with no names they retire quietly to
        a <strong>legacy</strong> shelf: out of your count, always visible under "Show all routines", and adding
        names revives them any time.</p>
      <p><strong>Someone else knows the cast better?</strong> Every routine card has <strong>✉️ Ask someone who
        knows</strong> — email a team parent or choreographer a private link that opens just that routine. They
        type the names (no account needed), you review with the usual preview before anything saves, and they're
        credited on the card for the help.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>7. Some awards my studio won aren't on our page — how do I add them?</h2>
      <p>If some awards your studio won aren't showing on your studio page, you can add them yourself — from the
        <strong>Studio History</strong> tab or the Quick-Add feature in your Studio Dashboard. It's usually one of
        two situations: the award was claimed by another studio by mistake, or it isn't in our database yet.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Search & Re-Claim:</strong> Search for the award by dancer or routine name. If it already exists
          in our database (from a previous competition import) — even if it landed on the wrong studio — you can
          click the "Claim" button next to it to bring it onto your roster.</li>
        <li><strong>Manual Entry:</strong> If the award is not yet in our database, you can click the <strong>+ Add
            Award</strong> button. You can manually enter all details including competition, date, awards, and
          placement.
          For efficiency, the system will remember the "Last Competition" you entered, pre-filling those details for
          subsequent entries.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Once added, the award will appear on your public dashboard and can be used in AI-generated summaries!</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>8. Why do dancer profile claims appear in my Verifications dashboard?</h2>
      <p>When someone claims a profile for a dancer on your roster, the claim comes to you rather than to the
        AwardHome team — with or without your <strong>Studio Claim Code</strong>. You know which families belong to
        which dancers; we have no way to check that from the outside, and a review that can't actually verify
        anything is worse than no review. You'll get an email when one arrives; open
        <strong>Pending Verifications → Profile Claims to Confirm</strong> and Confirm or Deny.</p>
      <p>The code, when used, only proves they're part of your studio community — please confirm the person
        actually belongs to <em>that dancer's</em> family before approving, since approval hands them full control
        of the profile. The claimant is emailed the decision automatically.</p>
      <p><strong>Approving may release more than the profile.</strong> A family waiting on a claim can already
        record awards, which are held privately until you decide — so confirming one claim can publish a whole
        weekend they entered while waiting. Denying it withdraws them. Two exceptions never reach you: if two
        households claim the same dancer, it goes to the AwardHome team instead (you should never be asked to
        choose between two of your families), and neither should a dancer who isn't on your roster.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>9. Can I control which statistics visitors see?</h2>
      <p>Yes — you have full control over what data visitors see on your public studio page. In the Management
        Dashboard, scroll down to the "Public Dashboard Visibility" section to toggle the following metric cards on
        or off:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Total Awards & Events Attended</li>
        <li>1st Place Finishes (All Time & This Year)</li>
        <li>Activity metrics (Past 5 Years & This Year)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>As an admin or verified owner, you will always be able to see all of your statistics. Cards that are hidden
        from the public will display a red <strong>HIDDEN</strong> badge for your eyes only.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>10. Can I curate our Hall of Fame?</h2>
      <p>The <strong>Hall of Fame</strong> on your public profile automatically showcases your studio's top achievements (such as 1st place titles, scholarships, and national finals placements). However, you have full control to override this and curate exactly what is shown.</p>
      <p>Go to your <strong>Studio History</strong> -> <strong>Awards</strong> editor. Next to each award, you will see a <strong>HoF</strong> column with a star icon. Click the icon to cycle through the visibility options:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>☆ Auto-Detect:</strong> Let the system decide if this award is prestigious enough for the Hall of Fame.</li>
        <li><strong style="color: #fbbf24;">⭐ Pinned:</strong> Force this award to always display in the Hall of Fame, regardless of placement or title.</li>
        <li><strong style="color: #ef4444;">🚫 Hidden:</strong> Force this award to never display in the Hall of Fame, even if it was a major win.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>11. Can I add photos and thank-you notes to my dancers' award cards?</h2>
      <p>Yes. Award cards can flip into a mini-book with a photo page and a thank-you page. As a
        studio director you can manage these for any dancer on your active roster: open <strong>Roster</strong> and
        click the <strong>Card</strong> button on a dancer's row (dancers and parents can also do it themselves from
        their Dancer Dashboard).</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Photos:</strong> each award can carry its own photo (usually the routine's performance shot),
          plus one default card photo per dancer as the fallback. Your first upload for a dancer asks for a
          one-time consent confirmation covering everyone pictured; later uploads skip it.</li>
        <li><strong>Thank-you notes:</strong> a short line per award (280 characters). On group routines every dancer
          gets their own line — teachers are usually the first ones thanked, so encourage your dancers to fill these
          in!</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Everything is reviewed by the AwardHome team before it appears publicly, and edits go back into review.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>12. Can I showcase our awards on my studio's own website?</h2>
      <p>Yes — natively, with the website widget! Go to the <strong>Widget Builder</strong> in
        your dashboard. You can customize the background and text colors to match your branding, and simply copy-paste
        the generated <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code> HTML code directly into your website builder (Wix, Squarespace,
        Wordpress, etc.).</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>13. Can AwardHome write our social media posts about competition results?</h2>
      <p>Yes — need to write a social media post or press release about your competition results but don't have the
        time? Our platform uses an advanced AI Marketing Summary Generator to do it for you instantly!</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Go to the <strong>Studio History</strong> tab in your dashboard.</li>
        <li>Click the <strong>📄 Generate Text Summary</strong> button next to the competition you want to highlight.
        </li>
        <li>Our system will automatically check the boxes for your major podium placements (1st, 2nd, 3rd, Hope, Grand
          Prix). You can manually check or uncheck any "Top 12/24" awards to completely curate your list.</li>
        <li>Choose your desired Tone ("Enthusiastic" for Instagram or "Professional" for Press Releases) and click
          <strong>✨ Generate AI Summary</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>The AI will instantly write a beautifully formatted, inspiring marketing blurb based <em>only</em> on the
          awards you selected. You can edit the text directly in the box if needed!</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>14. How can my studio get featured on the AwardHome Dance homepage?</h2>
      <p>Featured studios are selected automatically — no payment, no favoritism. To be eligible,
        a studio must be <strong>claimed</strong> and have a <strong>logo and bio</strong> on its profile. Among
        eligible studios, selection is based on recent, genuine engagement with your trophy case — things like adding
        new competition results and keeping your roster verified. Recent activity counts more than old activity.</p>
      <p>Two things worth knowing: repeating the same action over and over does <strong>not</strong> multiply your
        standing — the system rewards real upkeep, not volume. And slots rotate: a studio stays featured for a while,
        then rests so others get the spotlight. In short — genuinely maintain your studio's presence, and you'll get
        seen.</p>
      <p><strong>Being featured never changes your ranking.</strong> The leaderboards are decided by awards and
        nothing else, so a featured studio appears in both places — in the spotlight <em>and</em> at the position it
        earned. Losing a featured slot doesn't cost you a place on the board, and gaining one doesn't buy you one.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>15. Is there a way to see which competitions are coming up near us?</h2>
      <p>Yes — the <a href="/dance/events">Upcoming Events directory</a> gathers published tour
        dates from competitions across the platform in one place. Filter by <strong>state</strong>,
        <strong>month</strong>, or <strong>competition</strong> to map out your season without building the
        spreadsheet yourself — or tap <strong>"Near me"</strong> to sort by distance from you (your location is
        used for that page view only and never stored). Sign in to <strong>shortlist</strong> events with the
        star button, then export your shortlist — or any filtered view — straight into Google, Apple, or Outlook
        with <strong>"Add to Calendar (.ics)"</strong>. Follow each listing's registration link when you're
        ready to enter. Dates come from
        organizers' published schedules; always confirm dates and venues with the organizer before booking travel.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>16. A competition we attend isn't on AwardHome yet — can you add it?</h2>
      <p>Yes — and please tell us! Click <strong>Send Feedback</strong> in the header and
      name the competition (a link to its website helps). We'll work on bringing its results onto the
      platform and its tour dates into the <a href="/dance/events">Upcoming Events directory</a> — so your
      season planning and your studio's complete award record live in one place.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>17. What happens when dancers from two different studios compete together?</h2>
      <p>For a cross-studio Pas De Deux or Group (e.g., at YAGP), our system creates a "Pseudo-Studio" that literally
        combines both studio names (e.g., "Studio A & Studio B"). This keeps the database fast and prevents
        structural errors.</p>
      <p>Because it is technically a new combined entity, the award won't automatically appear on your main standalone
        dashboard. However, the award <strong>will</strong> perfectly appear on the individual Dancer's profile. If you
        want the award to also show up on your standalone Studio Widget, you can manually use the <strong>"Add
          Award"</strong> tool in your dashboard to self-report it onto your roster.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>18. My dashboard shows "Merge Suggestions" — what happens when I click Merge into Mine?</h2>
      <p>Competitions sometimes publish the same studio under slightly different names, so your awards can end up
        split across duplicate records. When we spot a likely match, it appears under <strong>Merge
        Suggestions</strong> on your dashboard, with its awards, years, and dancer names so you can judge whether
        it's really yours (same-named studios in different cities are common!).</p>
      <p>Clicking <strong>Merge into Mine</strong> sends a request to our team — every merge is double-checked by a
        human before it happens, which protects your record (and everyone else's) from mistaken or mischievous
        merges. Your request appears right away in the <strong>Merge Requests</strong> table on your dashboard as
        <em>Pending review</em>, and we email you the moment it's decided — approved merges bring every award from
        the duplicate onto your page. If a suggestion isn't your studio, <strong>Not My Studio</strong> hides it
        for good.</p>
      <p>Once a request is decided, you can tidy your dashboard with <strong>Clear decided &rarr; history</strong> —
        the full record always lives on your <strong>Action History</strong> page (linked from the Merge Requests
        panel), alongside a log of everything else that's happened on your studio.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>19. The roster page flagged "Suspected Duplicates" — how do I clean them up?</h2>
      <p>Competitions sometimes publish the same dancer under separate profiles, so one dancer can appear on your
        roster several times. The roster page groups same-name profiles and shows each one's claimed status, date
        of birth, and award count — and <strong>[View Profile]</strong> opens the full trophy case in a popup so
        you can compare without leaving the page.</p>
      <p>Every profile starts ticked. If they're all the same dancer, just hit <strong>Merge Selected</strong> —
        the claimed (or most-awarded) profile becomes the primary and every award follows it. If one of them is
        actually a different dancer, untick that profile first: it's left untouched, and after the merge you can
        merge the others separately or use <strong>Mark as Different People</strong> to stop the flag for that
        name. Handy when two real dancers share a name and each has a duplicate!</p>
      <p>And if no two of your students share a name at all, use <strong>merge all sets at once</strong> — the
        link above the list handles every set in one confirmed click. Sets with more than one claimed profile
        are left for you to review by hand, and names you marked as different people are never touched.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="faq-section">
    <h2>Families are adding awards — what do I do with them?</h2>
    <p>When a family adds an award we didn't have for one of your dancers, it goes to
      <strong>Family Submissions</strong> in your dashboard and nowhere else. Nothing is public until you
      confirm it. You can fix a placement or a routine spelling right there and confirm in one go — no need
      to reject a real award over a typo — or ask the family a question instead of deciding.</p>
    <p>Solos and group routines are recorded differently, using the size the family chose, so a group stays
      a group even when only one dancer is on it and teammates can add themselves later. If you previously
      removed a dancer from a routine, confirming will never quietly put them back.</p>
    <p>You only ever see submissions for your own studio's dancers.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="faq-section">
    <h2>Card photos now wait for me instead of AwardHome. How does that work?</h2>
    <p>A photo a family adds to an award card is visible straight away to the families of the dancers
      <em>in that routine</em>, and to nobody else. If none of them objects, you approve it in
      <strong>Pending Verifications</strong> and it goes public on that dancer's trophy case.</p>
    <p>If a family in the routine does object, the photo stops there and AwardHome decides it — you won't
      see an Approve button. That's on purpose: you shouldn't have to overrule one of your own families
      about a picture of their child.</p>
    <p>A dancer's default profile photo still goes through the AwardHome team, since it isn't tied to a
      routine and there's no cast to ask.</p>
  </section>

</main>

